        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Carbonnade is Belgium's answer to boeuf bourguignon. Where the French use wine, Belgians use their world-famous dark ales. The dish is eaten in every friterie and brasserie across Flanders. Some cooks add a slice of bread spread with mustard on top of the stew, which melts in and thickens the sauce. It is always served with frites, never without."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
